  "transcript": "# Proposals for backbench debates\n\n## The BBC’s future\n> Grahame Morris wanted MPs to discuss BBC cuts and the next charter before Christmas, with a Commons debate his first choice.\n\nChair: Let’s start with Grahame Morris’s request for a debate.\nGrahame Morris: I’m asking for a 90-minute debate on the BBC before Christmas. We expect the Government’s long-awaited White Paper by then, and the BBC is already making decisions about jobs and programmes that will shape the service under its next charter.\nGrahame Morris: MPs should be able to put their constituents’ views on record and ask Ministers what they plan to do. In my area, BBC Radio Newcastle and “Look North” show why local radio and regional news matter, especially to older people and those who can’t easily leave home.\nGrahame Morris: The broadcasting unions supporting the parliamentary group I’m involved in want urgent action on cuts, reliable funding for the BBC, separate and secure funding for the World Service, and a formal say for workers on the BBC’s board without compromising its editorial independence.\nGrahame Morris: Cuts to staff and programme-making affect what audiences can watch and listen to, as well as jobs and freelance work across the UK. A debate would let MPs question the Government’s choices before the next charter takes effect.\nWill Stone: A debate in the Commons Chamber needs at least 15 MPs’ names. Could you find four more supporters, or would you accept a debate in Westminster Hall?\nGrahame Morris: I’m fairly confident I can find more names. MPs from across parties have told me they’re interested. The Chamber is my first choice, but the most important thing is to hold the debate before Christmas. If there’s no Chamber time, I’d accept 90 minutes in Westminster Hall.\nAlison Hume: I sit on the DCMS Committee, which examines culture and media policy. I’m concerned that “You and Yours”, the only live Radio 4 programme made outside London, is being axed. My constituents value it. I support this request and hope the debate can happen soon.\nChair: The committee staff will contact you. If the debate is on a Tuesday morning, it must be on a day when the relevant Government department is due to answer.\n\n\n## A new autism strategy\n> Alison Hume sought a debate while the Government was preparing its next autism strategy, citing serious inequalities and limited progress so far.\n\nChair: Next is Alison Hume’s request for a debate.\nAlison Hume: I’m asking for a debate on what the Government’s next national autism strategy should prioritise. Autistic people still face severe inequalities: they die between six and 15 years earlier than the general population.\nAlison Hume: Only 26% of autistic students say they feel happy at school, and only three in 10 autistic adults are in work—one of the lowest employment rates for any disability.\nAlison Hume: The Autism Act 2009 requires the Government to produce and regularly update a strategy. But the House of Lords found that outcomes hadn’t improved despite those efforts. The current strategy expired at the end of June, and the Government says it will extend it while developing a replacement.\nAlison Hume: A debate now would let MPs from different parties contribute while the new strategy is being written. That matters as the Government introduces its new offer of support for children and young people with special educational needs and disabilities, known as SEND. I’m asking for Westminster Hall.\nMr Dillon: Those numbers are striking. Do you want the debate to coincide with a particular date, or would the next available slot work?\nAlison Hume: The next available slot would be fine.\nChair: I assume the Department of Health would answer the debate?\nAlison Hume: Yes, that’s right.\nChair: You’ve asked for a Thursday, so the restriction on Tuesday debates doesn’t apply. Committee staff will be in touch.\n",
